        <div id="how-much-protein-after-workout" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. How Much Protein Should You Take After a Workout?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The International Society of Sports Nutrition's 2017 position stand recommends 20 to 40 g of protein per dose, or about 0.25 g/kg, every three to four hours, within a daily total of 1.4 to 2.0 g/kg for people who train. The ACSM's joint position statement suggests about 0.3 g/kg after exercise. For a 70 kg person that is around 20 to 25 g after training; for a 90 kg lifter, closer to 30 g.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two newer findings push the useful dose higher than the old 20 g ceiling. Macnaughton and colleagues (2016) found that 40 g of whey stimulated more muscle protein synthesis than 20 g after a whole-body resistance session. And a 2023 study by Trommelen and colleagues in Cell Reports Medicine found that 100 g of milk protein after whole-body training produced a larger and much longer-lasting anabolic response than 25 g, over 12 hours. The practical lesson is not that you need 100 g shakes; it is that bigger doses are not wasted, so a generous serving after a hard full-body session is fine.
          </p>

          <p>
            Leucine is the amino acid that switches on muscle protein synthesis, and research suggests about 2 to 3 g per dose is enough to maximize the signal in most adults. Whey is about 10 to 11 percent leucine, so 20 to 25 g of whey delivers it. Plant proteins usually contain less, which is why plant-based lifters are advised to take slightly larger servings, around 30 g, or use blends.
          </p>

