        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Panicking about the 30-minute window</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The research shows total daily protein matters far more than drinking a shake within minutes of training. If you ate a protein-rich meal a few hours before, anything within the next couple of hours is fine. Save the urgency for fasted morning sessions and two-a-day training.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Taking too little per dose</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 10 g protein snack after training will not do much. Aim for 20 to 40 g, or about 0.3 to 0.4 g/kg, with 2 to 3 g of leucine. Bigger doses after a hard full-body session are not wasted, according to recent research, so err on the generous side.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Paying for hydrolysate you do not need</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Hydrolyzed whey absorbs a little faster, but studies have not shown it builds more muscle than isolate at the same dose. It is worth the premium for sensitive stomachs or two sessions a day. For everyone else, a good isolate or blend does the job.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting a powder to stop soreness</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Protein supports long-term repair and adaptation, but its effect on next-day soreness is small and inconsistent. Soreness falls as your body adapts to training. Sleep, enough calories and sensible progression make more difference than any recovery formula.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying creatine inside a blend</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Creatine works best at 3 to 5 g every day. Recovery blends often include it at lower or undisclosed doses. Buy plain creatine monohydrate separately and add it to your shake, so you know exactly how much you are getting.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
