            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>How long after a workout should I drink a protein shake?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Within a couple of hours is fine for most people, especially if you ate a protein-rich meal a few hours before training. If you trained fasted, have protein sooner. Research shows total daily protein matters more than the exact timing, so do not stress about the 30-minute window.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is hydrolyzed whey better for recovery?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Hydrolyzed whey absorbs slightly faster, but studies have not shown better long-term muscle gain or recovery compared with whey isolate at the same dose. It is worth considering if regular whey upsets your stomach or you train twice a day. Otherwise, isolate is just as effective and usually cheaper.
                </p>
              </div>
