            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Does protein powder help with sore muscles?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Only a little, if at all. Reviews of the research find small and inconsistent effects of protein on muscle soreness. Protein supports long-term repair and adaptation, which matters more. For soreness, sleep, enough calories, gradual training progression and time make the biggest difference.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Should I add creatine to my recovery shake?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Creatine monohydrate is one of the best-supported supplements, and the ISSN notes it may enhance recovery as well as strength. Take 3 to 5 g a day; timing is not critical, so adding it to your post-workout shake is a convenient habit. Check with a doctor first if you have kidney disease.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is tart cherry juice good for recovery?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Meta-analyses suggest small benefits for strength recovery and soreness, mostly when concentrate is taken twice daily for several days around a hard event. Because heavy antioxidant use might blunt training adaptations, it is better reserved for competition periods than used daily. It is not a substitute for adequate protein.
